<p>Hurricane damage claims in McAllen are less frequent than hail or wind claims, but when a tropical system arrives, the losses can be widespread. NOAA Storm Events records describe <strong>Hurricane Dolly</strong> (July 23, 2008) as leaving widespread minor to moderate structural damage across the Lower Rio Grande Valley, along with flooding in low-lying and poorly drained areas (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=129365" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI event record</a>). <strong>Hurricane Hanna</strong> made landfall on Padre Island as a Category 1 storm on July 25, 2020, then brought tropical-storm impacts and major rainfall flooding to Hidalgo County (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=914182" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI event record</a>).</p>
<p>Because hurricanes combine wind and water, they raise some of the most difficult coverage questions in property insurance. This page explains them for McAllen homeowners and businesses. See our <a href="/storm-history/">storm history</a> page for more local events.</p>

<h2>Types of Hurricane Damage Covered</h2>
<p>Coverage depends on what caused each piece of damage and which policy responds:</p>
