<h2>Insurance Settlement Considerations</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Named storm or hurricane deductibles</strong> are often a percentage of Coverage A. Check your declarations page.</li>
<li><strong>ACV vs. RCV:</strong> insurers typically pay actual cash value first and release recoverable depreciation after repairs. NFIP policies have their own valuation rules.</li>
<li><strong>Ordinance or law:</strong> rebuilding must meet McAllen&rsquo;s 2024 ICC codes, effective January 1, 2026. Upgrade costs are paid only to the extent ordinance or law coverage applies. See <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Limits:</strong> flood policies have their own building and contents limits, separate from your property policy.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Additional Living Expenses and Catastrophe Timelines</h2>
<p>After a hurricane, the practical question for many families is where to stay while repairs happen. If a covered peril such as wind makes the home unlivable, additional living expense (loss of use) coverage typically pays the <em>increase</em> in living costs, such as a rental or hotel, extra mileage, and meals above your normal grocery budget. It does not usually apply when the only damage is from flood, because flood policies generally do not include ALE. Keep every receipt and a simple log of normal versus storm-related expenses.</p>
<p>Timing also changes after a declared catastrophe. Under the Texas Prompt Payment of Claims Act, insurer deadlines to acknowledge, investigate, and decide claims are extended by 15 days after a weather-related catastrophe or major natural disaster declared by the insurance commissioner. Contractors, roofers, and mitigation companies are also in high demand, which can slow repairs and affect deadlines to recover depreciation. If repairs are delayed for reasons outside your control, tell your insurer in writing and keep a copy. The <a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Department of Insurance</a> publishes consumer guidance after major storms.</p>

<h2>How a Public Adjuster May Help</h2>
<p>A public adjuster in McAllen can review all of your policies, document wind and water damage separately, prepare a detailed estimate, track additional living expenses, and communicate with each insurer. For roofs, see <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage claims</a>; for interior water, see <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a>. We cannot guarantee a result. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

<h2>Policyholder Responsibilities</h2>
<ul>
<li>Report the loss to every insurer that may be involved as soon as possible.</li>
<li>Protect the property from further damage once it is safe, and keep receipts.</li>
<li>Photograph damaged items before discarding them for health or safety reasons.</li>
<li>Submit proofs of loss within each policy&rsquo;s deadline; NFIP proof of loss rules are strict.</li>
<li>Cooperate with inspections and document requests.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Applicable Policy Limitations and Exclusions</h2>
<p>Hurricane claims are commonly limited by flood and surface water exclusions, anti-concurrent causation clauses, named storm deductibles, interior rain provisions, mold sublimits, and debris removal caps. The NFIP excludes or limits certain items, such as many basement contents and additional living expenses. Contractual and statutory deadlines also apply. For legal questions, consult a Texas attorney.</p>
